# Ralph LOCAL config — copy this file to `config.local.sh` and edit.
#
#     cp .agents/ralph/config.local.sh.example .agents/ralph/config.local.sh
#
# `config.local.sh` is git-IGNORED, so it's the right place for anything
# machine- or person-specific: which agent binaries you have, what commands run
# them, and your preferred default roles. It is sourced LAST (after agents.sh,
# config.sh and review-config.sh), so whatever you set here wins over the shipped
# defaults. Nothing here is committed, so you won't get merge conflicts or leak
# your local setup.
#
# Precedence (highest first):  CLI flag  >  environment variable  >  THIS FILE  >
#                              config.sh / review-config.sh  >  agents.sh defaults
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1) Define a brand-new backend  (no code change needed)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A backend is just a command template named AGENT_<NAME>_CMD, where <NAME> is
# the backend name UPPERCASED with dashes turned into underscores. The template
# either contains {prompt} (replaced with a quoted prompt-file path) OR reads the
# prompt from stdin. Once defined, use it as any role: `--builder <name>`.
#
# Example: a custom "zlaude" backend (Claude Code wired to the Z.AI plan):
# AGENT_ZLAUDE_CMD='zlaude -p --dangerously-skip-permissions "$(cat {prompt})"'
#   ...then:  ralph batch --builder zlaude --reviewer codex-readonly ...
# The normalized `zai` provider below is preferred when you want to select any GLM
# model by name without defining one AGENT_*_CMD backend per model. Explicit custom
# backends like this remain supported.
# IMPORTANT for ANY claude-CLI-to-alternate-endpoint wrapper (zlaude/rlaude/...): the
# wrapper MUST `unset ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`. A stray key takes precedence over
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN, so the CLI silently authenticates to real Anthropic instead of
# your endpoint — the builder then runs detached from the worktree and reports "done"
# while changing nothing (this was the true root cause of #22).
#
# Self-hosted model on a rented GPU (vLLM/RunPod, etc.): write a wrapper script
# `rlaude` on your PATH that sets ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL / ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN and pins
# every ANTHROPIC_*_MODEL to the single served model (and `unset ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`),
# then `exec claude "$@"`. Keep the endpoint + key in the wrapper or an untracked env
# file — never here. If the server's Anthropic /v1/messages route doesn't work, point
# the wrapper at a local Anthropic->OpenAI proxy (LiteLLM / claude-code-router) instead;
# ralph is unaffected either way — it only sees the wrapper name.
# AGENT_RLAUDE_CMD='rlaude -p --dangerously-skip-permissions "$(cat {prompt})"'
#   ...then:  ralph batch --builder rlaude --reviewer rlaude ...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2) Override an existing backend's command
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Don't have codex? Point the name at whatever you use, or change its flags.
# opencode takes the message as an ARGV arg (NOT stdin), and you MUST pin a model your
# plan includes — the auto-default (glm-5.2-highspeed) 429s on plans without it and the
# SDK retry-loops on the 429, which looks like a hang / exit 124 (#44):
# AGENT_OPENCODE_Z_CMD='opencode run -m zai-coding-plan/glm-4.7 "$(cat {prompt})"'
# AGENT_CLAUDE_CMD='claude -p --dangerously-skip-permissions'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3) Set your default roles so you don't pass flags every time
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Use `: "${VAR:=value}"` (conditional) so an explicit --builder/--reviewer flag
# still overrides these. Plain `VAR=value` would beat the flag — usually not what
# you want.
# : "${BUILDER:=opencode-z}"
# : "${REVIEWER:=codex-readonly}"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4) Workflow defaults (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# : "${MAX_ITERATIONS:=15}"       # per-task builder/reviewer attempts in batch
# : "${CHECK_CMD:=./scripts/check.sh}"
# : "${VERDICT_REGEX:=^VERDICT: (PASS|FAIL)}"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5) Per-attempt token/cost usage (cost visibility on capped plans)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RALPH_USAGE makes instrumented builders/reviewers emit machine-readable usage in
# `ralph batch`, and writes a per-attempt sidecar next to each attempt log:
#   .agent-run/<run>/task-<NNN>-iter-<N>-<role>.usage.json
# with numeric input/output/cache_read/cache_creation tokens (plus num_turns,
# duration_ms and total_cost_usd where the backend reports them).
#
# ON BY DEFAULT — cost visibility is opt-OUT, not opt-in. Uncomment to disable on
# THIS machine:
# : "${RALPH_USAGE:=0}"
#
# Which backends are instrumented, and with what (see references/TOKEN_ECONOMICS.md):
#   claude family  --output-format json   cache_read_input_tokens / cache_creation_input_tokens
#   codex          --json (JSONL)         cached_input_tokens / cache_write_input_tokens
#   opencode       not instrumented       argv-only (no stdin); pin -m to a plan model (#44)
# Anything else is left untouched. The JSON is always extracted back to plain text
# before the verdict parse, so this never causes spurious reviewer ERRORs — including
# when a CLI changes its event names: an unrecognised shape is salvaged for its text
# (verdict still parses) and the harness warns that metrics were skipped.
#
# Covers the bare `claude` binary and claude-CLI wrappers (rlaude/zlaude), plus `codex`.
# Add other wrapper names if you use them:
#   RALPH_CLAUDE_LIKE="claude rlaude zlaude mylaude"
#   RALPH_CODEX_LIKE="codex mycodex"
# Note Z.AI reports cache READS only — `cache_creation` stays null on zlaude/Z.AI.
# (Self-hosted GPUs bill by GPU-second, not tokens, so the sidecars are for
# throughput/turns visibility there, not billing.)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6) Normalized agent selection — {provider, model, effort} per role (#4)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Instead of hand-writing AGENT_*_CMD strings, pick a provider/model/effort per
# role and let the harness compose the command. Precedence (high->low):
#   CLI flag  >  config.local.sh (here)  >  ralph.target.json "agents"  >  default
# A preset fills any knob you leave unset:
#   cheap    = builder codex/low     reviewer codex/low
#   balanced = builder codex/medium  reviewer codex/low
#   max      = builder codex/high    reviewer codex/medium
# : "${RALPH_PROFILE:=balanced}"
# Or set knobs directly (these win over a preset):
# : "${BUILDER_PROVIDER:=codex}"   ; : "${BUILDER_EFFORT:=high}"   # low|medium|high
# : "${REVIEWER_PROVIDER:=codex}"  ; : "${REVIEWER_EFFORT:=low}"
# : "${BUILDER_MODEL:=}"           ; : "${REVIEWER_MODEL:=}"       # empty = provider default
# Providers: codex (effort -> -c model_reasoning_effort), claude (model only; effort
# deferred), zai/zlaude (Z.AI model by name), opencode (model only), droid. Reviewer
# stays read-only automatically.
# Z.AI uses Claude Code with stdin and fully unsets ANTHROPIC_API_KEY plus the three
# ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_*_MODEL pins. Keep the token in this ignored file or your env;
# never commit it. The default base is the verified Anthropic-compatible route
# https://api.z.ai/api/anthropic (https://z.ai is not an API endpoint):
# : "${RALPH_ZAI_BASE_URL:=https://api.z.ai/api/anthropic}"
# : "${RALPH_ZAI_AUTH_TOKEN:=your-zai-token}"
# Then select any plan model directly, with no AGENT_ZLAUDE*_CMD model remap:
#   ralph batch ... --builder zai --builder-model glm-5.2 \
#     --reviewer zai --reviewer-model glm-4.5-air
# (`--builder-provider zai` / `--reviewer-provider zai` are equivalent normalized
# spellings. A defined AGENT_ZAI_CMD or AGENT_ZLAUDE_CMD still takes precedence.)
# Per-run override without editing this file:
#   RALPH_PROFILE=cheap ralph batch ...        BUILDER_EFFORT=high ralph batch ...
#   ralph batch --profile max --reviewer-effort low ...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7) Cron/orchestrator loop DRIVER — RALPH_CRON_DRIVER (#52)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The DRIVER is the agent that wakes on your recurring cadence (hourly cron, timer,
# session loop), reads ORCHESTRATOR.md and runs one pass. It is a THIRD role: setting
# it does NOT touch builder/reviewer selection above, and BUILDER/REVIEWER do not
# touch it. A driver script resolves it with `ralph_resolve_cron_driver` (agents.sh),
# which returns a command exactly the way the roles get theirs — so anything usable as
# a backend is usable as a driver.
#
# Either spelling works (the normalized spec wins if both are set):
# : "${RALPH_CRON_DRIVER:=codex}"                 # a backend name: codex, zlaude,
#                                                 # opencode-z, or your AGENT_<NAME>_CMD
# : "${RALPH_CRON_DRIVER_PROVIDER:=zai}"          # or a normalized {provider,model,effort}
# : "${RALPH_CRON_DRIVER_MODEL:=glm-4.5-air}"
# : "${RALPH_CRON_DRIVER_EFFORT:=low}"            # low|medium|high (codex maps it)
#
# Unset -> RALPH_CRON_DRIVER_DEFAULT (config.sh) -> $DEFAULT_AGENT, i.e. whatever this
# install already drives agents with. Repoint the default instead of hardcoding a
# vendor everywhere:
# : "${RALPH_CRON_DRIVER_DEFAULT:=opencode-z}"
#
# WHY THIS IS A KNOB, AND WHO OWNS IT: a loop pass is mechanical mid-tier throughput —
# read labels, dispatch the harness, run the Manager's acceptance verbatim, file a PR.
# Left hardcoded in a cron line, the driver is invisible and inherited: it silently
# burns a frontier-priced pool doing clerical work. So the ORCHESTRATOR owns this
# choice (see ORCHESTRATOR.md) and revisits it when conditions change — a credential
# pool near saturation, a cheaper model shipping, a free tier opening. Favour the
# CHEAPEST COMPETENT driver, and keep it on a pool the builder/reviewer are not
# already saturating; it need not be the model of the session that configured it.
# 7) Per-provider pricing table (USD per million tokens, for `ralph report`)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The default pricing table ships at .agents/ralph/pricing.json. To override
# rates (they change), copy it, edit the copy, and point RALPH_PRICING_FILE at
# your copy. Or set a custom path here:
# : "${RALPH_PRICING_FILE:=/path/to/my-pricing.json}"
#
# Schema (pricing.json):
#   {
#     "providers": {
#       "anthropic":  { "input": 3.00, "output": 15.00, "cache_read": 0.30, "cache_write": 3.75 },
#       "openai":     { "input": 2.50, "output": 10.00, "cache_read": 1.25, "cache_write": 2.50 },
#       "zai":        { "input": 1.40, "output": 5.60,  "cache_read": 0.26, "cache_write": 0.26 },
#       "deepseek":   { "input": 0.27, "output": 1.10,  "cache_read": 0.07, "cache_write": 0.27 }
#     },
#     "aliases": {
#       "claude": "anthropic",
#       "codex": "openai",
#       "zlaude": "zai",
#       "opencode-z": "zai",
#       "dlaude": "deepseek"
#     }
#   }
# All rates are USD per MILLION tokens. `cost_usd` = tokens × rate / 1_000_000.
# Unknown providers (not in providers or aliases) -> cost_usd = "unknown".
